From: mpc-pi
Subject: [PATCH] sound: raise the effects thread above the emulation thread

The effects thread is the audio producer. run_effects() is what calls
osd().sound_stream_sink_update(), so every sample the host ever hears passes
through this thread and no other. It is created as a plain std::thread, which
inherits the creating thread's scheduling policy and priority.

That is fine when the process is SCHED_OTHER. It is fatal when the process is
realtime and pinned. This appliance launches MAME as

    taskset --cpu-list 3 nice -n .. chrt --rr 20 env .. mame ..

so the emulation thread AND the effects thread are both SCHED_RR 20 on one
core. Under SCHED_RR a thread that wakes at EQUAL priority does not preempt the
running one - it waits for that thread to block or to exhaust its timeslice, and
/proc/sys/kernel/sched_rr_timeslice_ms is 100 on this kernel. The emulation
thread is runnable almost continuously, so the effects thread ran only when
emulation happened to block. /proc/<tid>/schedstat over 10 seconds:

    emulation thread   ran 1127.5 ms, waited on the runqueue    0.3 ms
    effects thread     ran   12.2 ms, waited on the runqueue 1013.3 ms

The result is not jitter, it is loss. Each run of run_effects() hands over only
the samples of the current round; rounds that the thread is not scheduled for
are never handed over at all. Measured as a fraction of realtime, the producer
delivered 9.7%. The OSD ring padded the shortfall by repeating the last sample
and the PipeWire module's prebuffer path memset whole buffers, so a capture of
what reached the DAC was 84% digital silence - while MAME's own -wavwrite, which
streams_update() writes on the EMULATION thread upstream of all this, was
perfectly clean. That divergence is what made the fault so hard to place.

MAME_SOUND_EFFECTS_PRIORITY sets the effects thread's priority. On a realtime
policy it stays on that policy and takes the given priority; the default is the
creating thread's priority plus 40, clamped to the policy maximum, which is
enough to preempt and nowhere near the graph's own threads. Absent or set to 0
the thread is left exactly as before, so behaviour off this appliance is
unchanged.

Measured on the Raspberry Pi 5 appliance at a 64-frame quantum, audio produced
per second of wall clock as a fraction of realtime:

    effects thread at emulation priority     9.7%
    effects thread raised                   99.8%, underruns 0, overruns 0

This supersedes doing the same thing from outside with chrt after start, which
has to identify the emulation thread by CPU share and cannot run until the
threads exist.

diff --git a/src/emu/sound.cpp b/src/emu/sound.cpp
--- a/src/emu/sound.cpp
+++ b/src/emu/sound.cpp
@@ -29,6 +29,12 @@
 
 #include <algorithm>
 #include <cstdlib>
+#include <cstring>
+
+#if defined(__linux__)
+#include <pthread.h>
+#include <sched.h>
+#endif
 
 //**************************************************************************
 //  DEBUGGING
@@ -51,6 +57,65 @@
 
 namespace {
 
+// Put the effects thread above the thread that created it.
+//
+// run_effects() is the audio producer - it is the only caller of
+// osd().sound_stream_sink_update(), so every sample the host hears passes
+// through this thread and no other. It runs on a std::thread, which inherits
+// the creating thread's policy and priority, so when MAME is started under
+// `chrt --rr N` the producer ends up AT the emulation thread's priority.
+//
+// Under SCHED_RR a thread that wakes at equal priority does not preempt the
+// running one; it waits for that thread to block or to exhaust its timeslice.
+// The emulation thread is runnable almost continuously, so on a pinned core the
+// producer runs only when emulation happens to block - and the audio it did not
+// hand over is not delayed, it is lost, because each round of run_effects()
+// hands over only the samples of that round.
+//
+// Raising it is safe: this thread does a small, bounded amount of work per
+// round and then blocks on a condition variable, so it cannot starve emulation.
+void raise_effects_thread(std::thread &thread)
+{
+#if defined(__linux__)
+	char const *const setting = std::getenv("MAME_SOUND_EFFECTS_PRIORITY");
+	int policy = SCHED_OTHER;
+	sched_param param{};
+	if(pthread_getschedparam(pthread_self(), &policy, &param) != 0)
+		return;
+	if((policy != SCHED_FIFO) && (policy != SCHED_RR))
+		return;  // no realtime policy inherited, so nothing to fix
+
+	int wanted = param.sched_priority + 40;
+	if(setting) {
+		const long requested = std::strtol(setting, nullptr, 10);
+		if(requested <= 0)
+			return;  // explicitly disabled
+		wanted = int(requested);
+	}
+	const int maximum = sched_get_priority_max(policy);
+	if(wanted > maximum)
+		wanted = maximum;
+	if(wanted <= param.sched_priority)
+		return;
+
+	sched_param raised{};
+	raised.sched_priority = wanted;
+	const int error = pthread_setschedparam(thread.native_handle(), policy, &raised);
+	if(error)
+		osd_printf_warning(
+				"Sound: could not raise the effects thread to priority %d: %s\n",
+				wanted,
+				std::strerror(error));
+	else
+		osd_printf_verbose(
+				"Sound: effects thread raised from priority %d to %d\n",
+				param.sched_priority,
+				wanted);
+#else
+	(void)thread;
+#endif
+}
+
 // Host sound-update cadence.
 //
 // STREAMS_UPDATE_FREQUENCY is a fixed rate, so the number of frames carried by
@@ -311,7 +376,10 @@
 	if(m_nosound_mode)
 		m_effects_thread = nullptr;
 	else
+	{
 		m_effects_thread = std::make_unique<std::thread>([this]{ run_effects(); });
+		raise_effects_thread(*m_effects_thread);
+	}
 #endif
 }
 
